--- body ---
## Goal

Every review phase ends within its time budget with a verdict, or with a
recorded "no report". This bounds the worst case and is directly checkable.
Reducing the variability of review time is the main lever; whether the
startup wait is a fixed cost or variability is settled by measurement.

## Why

Raids 1080 and 1089 overran the author's 20-minute closing window: 79 and 59
minutes from first review to merge (PRs 1389, 1390). The time went to seats
that failed to start, stalled or were dropped, not to reviewing itself.
Unbroken reviews are already fast (JETP-observer #154: 1.8 min; #152:
10.6 min). Evidence and counts: docs/discussions/2026-10-14-1096-design.md
("What the evidence says about the 20-minute window") and its seat records.

## Sequencing

Blocked by 1100 (implement the simplified review layers: gaze becomes the single review
skill, no second battery). Budgets are set on the simplified chain, not on
the layers 1100 removes. Design ticket 1096 is closed.

## Scope

1. Measure first. Timestamp every seat: launched, started, finished, failed
   or timed out, including failed starts (today only Codex seats leave a
   trace, through UUIDv7 session ids). Break one raid's closing time down
   by phase. Current derived estimate: ~10 min before the first native seat
   starts, ~15 min lost to a timed-out gate, ~17 min to completion.
2. Fix the startup failures recorded on 1389/1390: the Claude CLI argument
   error at launch, the thread limit when spawning native seats, native
   review failing on a read-only file system.
3. Bound timeouts: a budget per seat and per gate; on expiry, record
   "no report" and move on, instead of discovering an exit-124 controller
   or an interrupted gate later.
4. Stop the isolation guard from dropping seats (7 PRs, the most frequent
   recorded cause). Find why it refuses legitimate seats; prefer narrowing
   or deleting a misfiring guard over growing it (rules/workflow.md).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Per-phase timestamps, failed starts included, are recorded for every
      review phase and seat.
- [ ] Each review phase and seat has a budget; on expiry it records
      "no report" rather than waiting.
- [ ] The startup failures listed above no longer reproduce.
- [ ] The isolation guard no longer drops legitimate seats, or is narrowed.
- [ ] A full raid closes from first review to merge within 20 minutes,
      shown by its timestamps, phase by phase.
- [ ] No seat is dropped silently: a missing seat appears as "no report",
      and a required one still blocks the merge (ESCALATE).


## Author extension — test and validation waiting (2026-10-15)

The author explicitly confirmed that Imperial Dragon Harness must also address
time spent testing, following the climate-finance-het and JETP Observer
discussion. Scope is total development feedback latency, not reviewer runtime
alone. This extension records work, not a gate-policy change.

### Evidence already available

memory/topics/ci-and-test-suite.md records historical full-suite improvement
from 81 s to 33 s, a 1 h 49 min stuck forge check (cause unknown), repeated
local CI calls, and a ticket-only change taking over 13 minutes without
per-step timing. These are dated observations, not current benchmarks.
Makefile already separates check-fast, lint and full check; full pytest uses
four workers when xdist is present. Local make check does not establish
equivalence with the workflow's complete job set.

### Phases

1. Measure a representative contribution end to end: environment/bootstrap,
   test collection and execution, lint/adherence, local CI, forge queue,
   reviewer startup/collection, caller resumption and promotion/merge.
   Include failed/interrupted attempts. Separate useful execution from
   avoidable waiting; update historical timings before promising gains.
2. Map touched components and shared dependencies to existing validations.
   Pilot the smallest sufficient selection, reuse exact-revision evidence
   where valid and remove measured duplicate runs. Do not create another
   blanket gate, polling loop or configuration layer without demonstrated need.
3. Evaluate batching expensive integration checks on immutable candidate
   commits while agents continue working, then promoting only the validated
   snapshot. Retain proportionate gaze and targeted tests before integration.
   This is an option to evaluate alongside ticket 1045, not authorization to
   bypass current required checks or deploy integration/main semantics.
4. Demonstrate the selected pilot and compare total feedback/merge latency,
   repeated runs and waiting with baseline; roll out in bounded child PRs.

The measurement of existing test/CI waiting can start independently of 1100.
The original reviewer-budget implementation still follows 1100; split a child
ticket if needed rather than weakening that dependency silently.

### Additional exit criteria

- [ ] Current timings identify test execution, duplicate runs, queues and
      orchestration idle time separately, including failures.
- [ ] A representative pilot reduces demonstrated unnecessary waiting or
      repeated work with no loss of relevant checks.
- [ ] Shared dependency/configuration changes expand test selection correctly;
      stale or missing evidence never counts as a pass.
- [ ] Local-versus-forge coverage and reuse/promotion rules are explicit;
      policy changes are reviewed before use.
- [ ] Results report elapsed feedback time as well as CPU/test time; no
      unmeasured speedup or new numerical budget is asserted.
